    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Landscape edging is often thought of as the finishing touch of a well-designed garden: the detail that defines borders, contains gravel and stops grass creeping into flower beds. Among the materials available, steel stands out for its durability, slim profile and modern look. Yet many homeowners treat steel edging as a simple "set it and forget it" job, and that assumption leads to buying and installation errors that can turn a weekend project into an expensive one.</p>
<p>When steel edging fails, it does not just look untidy. It can damage mowers and strimmers, create trip hazards and, at worst, need to be dug out and replaced. Understanding material thickness, soil conditions and how the system is fixed together is the best way to get it right the first time.</p>

<h2 id="thickness">1. Thickness</h2>
<p>The most common mistake is choosing steel that is too thin to save on upfront cost. Steel thickness is sometimes quoted as a US gauge number, where a higher gauge means thinner metal, so it is clearer to compare products by their thickness in millimetres. Thin strips, well under 1.5 mm (roughly 16 gauge or thinner), are easy to carry and bend, but they often lack the stiffness to cope with a living landscape.</p>
<p>Over time, the weight of soil, frost heave and the occasional knock from a boot or mower wheel can make thin steel warp and buckle, and once it has lost its line it is very hard to straighten neatly. Many landscape-grade steel edgings are around 1.5–3 mm (roughly 16 to 11 gauge) or thicker, which gives a much more rigid, crisp edge. There is no single correct thickness for every garden, so check the manufacturer's specification and match it to your soil, the length of the runs and how tight your curves are.</p>

<h2 id="installation-depth">2. Installation depth</h2>
<p>Getting the height wrong is the mistake that most often leads to damaged equipment. If the edging stands too proud, it becomes a target for mower blades. A single strike can chew up the edging, blunt or bend the blade and put strain on the mower itself.</p>
<p>Set too shallow, the edging fails at its main job: stopping grass roots. Spreading grasses with creeping runners, such as couch grass (in warmer US climates, Bermuda or zoysia), will simply grow underneath a shallow edge and into your beds. Follow the manufacturer's guidance on burial depth; as a rough guide, the edging should go deep enough to stop grass roots, often 5–8 cm (2–3 in) or more below ground. Leave the top edge a little above the soil, about 1 cm or so, and low enough for the mower blade to clear, then set your mower height accordingly.</p>

<!--figure:pebbles-meeting-concrete-edge|Set deep enough, an edge keeps two surfaces apart along a clean, straight line.-->

<h2 id="corrosion-protection">3. Corrosion protection</h2>
<p>Steel and moisture do not mix well unless the steel is chosen and finished for outdoor use. Many homeowners buy raw steel assuming it will develop an attractive rusty look on its own, but there is a real difference between weathering steel and ordinary carbon steel.</p>
<p>Ordinary carbon steel can keep rusting until it is weakened and starts to break down in the soil, and acidic or salty soil speeds the process up. Weathering steel is designed to form a stable rust-coloured surface layer that slows further corrosion, while galvanised or powder-coated steel relies on its coating for protection. Choosing the wrong one, such as uncoated steel in a wet border or a painted product when you wanted a rusted finish, leads to early failure. Bear in mind too that weathering steel can stain nearby paving or pale gravel, especially while the surface is first developing, so think about where run-off will go.</p>
<p>Replacing rusted-out edging is hard, slow work. Old strips can have jagged, sharp edges, so wear sturdy gloves, lift them out carefully rather than yanking them free, and dispose of the metal safely, ideally through your local recycling centre's scrap metal facility.</p>

<h2 id="joint-connectivity">4. Joint connectivity</h2>
<p>A steel edging run is only as strong as its weakest point, and that is almost always the joint where two sections meet. Some lighter products rely on flimsy clip-on connectors or simple overlaps held together by friction.</p>
<p>As the ground freezes and thaws, the soil expands and contracts and puts real pressure on those joints. Weak connections can pull apart and leave gaps that look untidy and let grass and weeds through. A section that springs loose can also lift into a sharp, raised edge, which is a hazard for children and pets. Better systems use interlocking tabs or sleeves, with a stake driven through both pieces at each joint, so the line stays continuous and secure as the soil moves.</p>

<h2 id="curvature-planning">5. Curvature planning</h2>
<p>Forcing straight steel into tight curves without a plan is an easy way to ruin good material. Bent beyond what it can take, or without the right technique, steel kinks. A kink is a permanent weak point and spoils the flowing line that made you choose steel in the first place.</p>
<p>Match the thickness of the steel to the curves in your design. Very thick steel, around 6 mm (¼ in), is excellent for straight runs and formal shapes but generally needs a bender to form curves. For sweeping beds, a thinner landscape-grade strip, within the range the manufacturer recommends for curves, usually offers a better balance of flexibility and strength. Mark the curve out on the ground first, bend gradually along its length rather than at one point, and check the manufacturer's minimum radius before you buy.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Lay a garden hose along the planned line and live with it for a day or two. It shows you exactly how tight each curve is, which makes it much easier to choose a steel thickness that will follow it without kinking.</p></blockquote>

<!--figure:curved-gravel-drive-lawn-edge|Plan long, gentle curves; tight bends are hard to form neatly in steel.-->

<h2 id="anchoring-systems">6. Anchoring systems</h2>
<p>Even good-quality steel will fail if it is not anchored properly. The usual mistakes are too few stakes, or stakes that are too short for the soil. In loose or sandy soil, short stakes can pull out, letting the edging lean or float over time.</p>
<p>Once the edging starts to tilt, it stops holding back mulch or gravel, which then spreads onto the lawn after heavy rain and has to be raked back or topped up. Follow the manufacturer's guidance on stake length and spacing, put a stake at every joint, and use longer stakes in loose or sandy soil so they reach firmer ground below. That keeps the edging upright and doing its job.</p>

<h2 id="doing-it-right">7. Why doing it right pays off</h2>
<p>Choosing a suitable thickness and finish, and taking care over depth, joints, curves and stakes, usually costs a little more at the outset than the cheapest option. In return you are far less likely to face repairs, lost mulch and gravel, damaged mowers or a full replacement, and the edging will keep its clean line for much longer. For most gardens, getting it right once works out better than redoing it later.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Is steel edging better than plastic or wood?</h3>
<p>For many gardens, yes, in terms of durability and looks. Plastic can become brittle in sunlight, and timber eventually rots. Steel gives a much thinner, cleaner line and, installed well, typically outlasts both. Lifespan depends on the product, soil, climate and care, so check the manufacturer's warranty.</p>
<h3>Will rust from steel edging harm my plants?</h3>
<p>A surface patina is normal on many steel products and is not generally considered harmful to garden plants. The bigger concern is choosing a steel that will hold up, such as weathering, galvanised or powder-coated steel, so it does not break down and leave sharp fragments in the soil.</p>
<h3>How do I stop the edging heaving out of the ground?</h3>
<p>Bury it to the depth the manufacturer recommends, use enough stakes of a suitable length for your soil, and firm the soil well on both sides. The weight of compacted soil on either side helps hold the strip in place.</p>
<h3>Can I mow over steel edging?</h3>
<p>If the top sits only a little above the soil, most mowers can pass over it. Even so, set the cutting height so the blade clears the edging, and take care with strimmers along the line.</p>
<h3>Is heavier steel worth the extra cost?</h3>
<p>Thicker landscape-grade steel costs more than thin strips or plastic, but it holds its line far better and is less likely to need replacing, so for most borders it is the better long-term choice. Very thick steel is best kept for straight or gently curved runs.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most steel edging problems come down to a few decisions made before and during installation: the right thickness and finish, the right depth, secure joints, curves the steel can actually follow, and enough stakes for your soil. Get those right and the edging will frame your borders cleanly for many seasons. Once it is in, our guide to <a href="/blog/steel-edging-maintenance-by-season">steel edging maintenance by season</a> will help keep it that way. If you are still deciding on a material, compare the options in <a href="/blog/landscape-edging-pros-and-cons">landscape edging pros and cons</a>, or follow our step-by-step guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-build-landscape-edging">how to build landscape edging</a>. For help designing a garden or terrace where every edge, path and border works together, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
